WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.080 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  [Music]  the focus in the United States on  research ethics began with the news of  the research done by Nazi physicians on  concentration camp prisoners during  World War two there was a war crimes  trial held in the city of Nuremberg  which produced the Nuremberg code the  first international code of ethics for  research on human subjects in the United  States interest in the topic was  reawakened in the mid to late 1960s when  we got reports of unethical research  being done including the Tuskegee  syphilis studies some of the key cases  that attracted the attention of the  United States Congress to the issue they  studied quite a number of other  suspected cases and in response to this  created a National Commission called the  National Commission for the protection  of human research subjects one of its  most famous products was the so-called  Belmont report I personally as a  consultant to the Commission was the  co-author of the Belmont report with Tom  Beecham another consultant from  Georgetown University we had no idea  when we wrote this thing of what  influence it would have we thought it  might guide the development of  regulations in the United States but  instead it became the standard  declaration of ethical principles for  research not only in the in the United  States but  in most of the rest of the world but  although we must be evolving our ethical  standards in order to be responsive not  only to the technological but other  standards evolution of other  considerations relevant to the time but  we have to be on guard against what I  call the appearance of infinite  malleability that is will change the  ethical codes any way that's necessary  in order to allow the science to go  forward that's something that I believe  we cannot do we cannot simply be  responsive to the technological demands  of scientific development but we must  give them due consideration and come out  with standards that are responsive to  all the relevant considerations of the  time and place  [Music]

